Answer: The Plane class contains two methods:

```java
class Plane {
```

void fly() {

```java
System.out.println("Plane is flying");
}
```

void land() {

```java
System.out.println("Plane is landing");
}
}
```

The fly() method provides a general flying behavior, while land() provides a general landing behavior.

Answer: CargoPlane extends Plane. It uses the inherited fly() method, overrides land(), and adds the specialized carryCargo() method.

```java
class CargoPlane extends Plane {
```

void land() {

```java
System.out.println("Cargo plane is landing on huge runway");
}
```

void carryCargo() {

```java
System.out.println("Cargo plane is carrying cargo");
}
}
```

The land() implementation is changed because the landing requirement of a cargo plane is different.

Answer: The Plane class already provides a land() method, but CargoPlane provides its own implementation: void land() {

```java
System.out.println("Cargo plane is landing on huge runway");
}
```

The method name remains land(), but its implementation changes according to the requirement of CargoPlane.

